<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Minyue</h1><p class="st-meta">202 BCE – 110 BCE (approx.) · capital Dongye</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Min Yue people in Fujian, ruled by descendants of Goujian of Yue, which Han armies destroyed in 110 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Min Yue were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. Their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zou-wuzhu">Wuzhu</a>, who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">King Goujian</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-yue">State of Yue</a>, had been demoted under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> but supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>. In 202 BCE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> recognised him as King of Minyue, with his capital at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dongye">Dongye</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Minyue was a coastal and mountain kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers, fishermen and seafarers. The walled Han-period city at Chengcun near Wuyishan shows a mixture of Han and Yue building styles. Its kings expanded aggressively, attacking <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dongou">Dong'ou</a> in 138 BCE and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanyue">Nanyue</a> in 135 BCE, when King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zou-ying">Zou Ying</a> was killed by his own brother Yushan to avert a Han invasion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 111 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zou-yushan">Zou Yushan</a> declared himself emperor and attacked Han forces. Han armies invaded by land and sea, his followers killed him, and in 110 BCE the Han moved much of the population north to the Yangtze-Huai region and abolished the kingdom.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/minyue.jpg" alt="Minyue" width="320" height="313"><figcaption>Image: Perinbaba · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minyue.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>202 BCE – 110 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dongye</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wuzhu, Zou Ying, Zou Yushan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Yue local religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yue languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Min Yue</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-156&amp;focus=minyue" aria-label="Minyue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7389 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Minyue lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7709 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7709 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-156&amp;focus=minyue">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

